<title>USA: World Bank sees Latin America inflation waning.</title>
<headline>World Bank sees Latin America inflation waning.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-10-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>The World Bank's Latin America and Caribbean division released Wednesday its lead economists' working numbers on inflation for the region.</p>
<p>The following is a table of those non official projections for annual changes in consumer prices indices, in percentages.</p>
<p> COUNTRY		1996	   1997	   1998</p>
<p> Argentina	    1.0	    1.5	    2.0</p>
<p> Belize		 2.4	    2.5	    2.5</p>
<p> Bolivia	     11.8	    6.5	    5.5</p>
<p> Brazil		13.0	   15.0	   15.0</p>
<p> Chile		  7.3	    6.5	    5.5</p>
<p> Colombia	    21.0	   20.0	   14.0</p>
<p> Costa Rica	  12.5	   12.5	   10.0</p>
<p> Dominican Rep	6.0	   10.0	    8.0</p>
<p> Ecuador	     19.5	   18.0	   17.4</p>
<p> El Salvador	 10.0	    9.0	    8.5</p>
<p> Guatemala	   10.0	   11.0	    9.0</p>
<p> Guyana		 7.7	    4.3	    3.6</p>
<p> Haiti		 20.0	   14.9	    7.0</p>
<p> Honduras	    15.0	    8.0	    4.5</p>
<p> Jamaica	     22.5	   15.0	   12.0</p>
<p> Mexico		27.0	   12.5	    9.0</p>
<p> Nicaragua	   11.0	    9.5	    8.0</p>
<p> Panama		 1.3	    1.3	    1.3</p>
<p> Paraguay	    12.0	    9.0	    9.0</p>
<p> Peru		  10.0	    7.0	    5.0</p>
<p> Trinidad	     4.2	    3.5	    3.1</p>
<p> Uruguay	     25.9	   15.0	   12.0</p>
<p> Venezuela	   86.7	    8.6	    4.0</p>
<p>-- IMF newsroom (202) 842-2124</p>
</text>
